## The 1.74ct Sub-Threshold Jump — Why 0.01ct Saves $900

**The 1.74ct Sub-Threshold Jump is a pricing-tier arbitrage specific to the 1.75ct line.** Diamonds price in quarter-carat tiers. 1.50–1.74ct is one tier. 1.75–1.99ct is the next tier up. Crossing from 1.74 to 1.75 triggers a 7–10% price step. The visual difference is 0.1 mm of length. Nobody sees it.

### The Math on 1.74 vs 1.75

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>1.74ct Marquise</th><th>1.75ct Marquise</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Length (mm)</strong></td><td>12.9</td><td>13.0</td><td>0.1 mm difference. Invisible.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Width (mm)</strong></td><td>6.45</td><td>6.5</td><td>0.05 mm narrower. Nobody will measure.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Face-up area (mm²)</strong></td><td>65.3</td><td>66.4</td><td>1.7% less. Falls inside normal viewing variance.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Typical price (G-VS1 Ideal)</strong></td><td>~$11,900</td><td>~$12,800</td><td>~$900 saved for 0.01ct nobody can measure.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Certificate reads</strong></td><td>"1.74 ct"</td><td>"1.75 ct"</td><td>The weight line is the only real difference.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** 1.74ct is the smart sub-threshold play at this tier. Nobody introduces their ring as "1.74 carat." The ring looks identical to a 1.75ct marquise on every finger. The $900 saving funds a full color grade upgrade or a halo band.

### How to Find Sub-Threshold Stones

Use a carat range filter instead of locking at 1.75ct. Set the carat filter to **1.68–1.76** instead of 1.75. Sort by price ascending. The cheapest stones in that range are usually 1.70–1.74ct and the saving is pure.

I use this filter on every 1.75ct marquise brief. In the natural market, where inventory is already paper-thin, dropping to 1.72–1.74 can also open up stones that the strict 1.75 filter misses entirely. The pool of available stones doubles. The price per stone drops $800–$1,100.

> **"The 1.74ct marquise is the stealth buy at this tier. I show clients both the 1.74 and the 1.75 side by side on video. Not one has ever been able to pick which is which. The $900 saving goes toward a platinum setting instead of 14k gold — a visible upgrade on the hand."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

## The SI Clarity Trap at 1.75ct Natural

**The SI Clarity Trap is specific to 1.75ct natural marquise.** Both certified natural stones in current market inventory sit at SI1 or SI2 clarity. On a marquise this size (13 × 6.5 mm face-up), SI inclusions have a high chance of being eye-visible — the long parallel step facets act like windows into the stone.

### Why 1.75ct Natural Market Is SI-Heavy

Cutters producing natural marquise target 1.5ct and 2.0ct for commercial reasons. A 1.75ct marquise is often a stone that could not quite make 2.0ct and gets re-aimed at the 1.75 tier. These stones usually come with trade-offs — SI1 or SI2 clarity because the inclusion limits where it could be cut cleanly to 2ct+.

The result in current inventory: both natural 1.75ct stones are SI clarity, both are Very Good cut (not Ideal), both carry per-carat prices over $7,000. The two stones:

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>Price</th><th>Clarity Risk</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>G-SI2 Very Good</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29498926?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$12,804</a></td><td>High — SI2 on marquise is often eye-visible</td><td>Only buy after HD video review. Center-of-table inclusion rejects.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>E-SI1 Very Good</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29904318?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$13,564</a></td><td>Medium — SI1 sometimes eye-clean, sometimes not</td><td>Better clarity + colour than G-SI2. Still mandatory HD video check.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Both natural 1.75ct stones require HD video review before purchase. Inclusions in the center of the table at SI2 are a hard reject on marquise. SI1 is sometimes eye-clean, sometimes not — case-by-case. If you want VVS clarity on natural at 1.75ct, the inventory does not exist right now — step up to 2ct natural or step down to 1.5ct natural.

### How to Vet an SI Marquise

Open the HD video on the retailer listing. Enlarge the stone face-up. Scan for dark spots, white clouds, or black carbon marks in the center-of-table zone. Any visible inclusion there is a reject. Edge-only inclusions near the tips are tolerable because a V-prong setting hides them.

I run this scan on every SI marquise before I quote a client. Three out of four SI1 stones pass. One out of three SI2 stones pass. The data confirms the trap — SI2 at 1.75ct is a buyer-beware zone.

