**Verdict:** The matrix shows one pattern. Lab wins on every objective metric at 1.75ct — the Lab Quadruplet floor is unprecedented value. Natural is only honest for buyers with $14k+ ring budgets who want GIA + natural + E colour specifically and who can verify SI1 eye-clean on video.

## 1.75 Carat Marquise by Finger Size

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Ring Size</th><th>Finger Width (mm)</th><th>Lengthwise Coverage</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Size 4</strong></td><td>14.5</td><td>90%</td><td>Overpowering. 1.75ct marquise is too much stone for petite fingers.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Size 5</strong></td><td>15.5</td><td>84%</td><td>Bold statement. Approaches the knuckle. Consider halo for balance.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Size 6</strong></td><td>16.5</td><td>79%</td><td>Our sweet spot. 1.75ct reads as 2.25ct round here.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Size 7</strong></td><td>17.3</td><td>75%</td><td>Strong fit. Reads as confident ring. Universal good choice.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Size 8</strong></td><td>18.2</td><td>71%</td><td>Great fit. 1.75ct suits size 8 well without needing halo.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Size 9+</strong></td><td>19.0+</td><td>68% or less</td><td>Balanced. 1.75ct length works with wider fingers.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** 1.75ct marquise fits size 5 through size 9+ cleanly. Size 4 can look dominated — step down to 1.5ct or add a halo. Size 6 is the honest sweet spot — 79% lengthwise coverage reads as a confident ring that doesn't cross the knuckle.

## Pros and Cons of 1.75 Carat Marquise

**Pros:**

- **The Lab Quadruplet.** Four identical E-VVS2 stones at $1,638 is unprecedented value.
- **Lab actually cheaper than 1.5ct lab.** Inventory anomaly — 1.75ct lab is $183 less than 1.5ct lab right now.
- **Reads as 2.25ct round face-up.** Elongation Illusion delivers one and a quarter carat tiers up in perceived size.
- **Fits size 5–9+ cleanly.** Universal good finger fit for most buyers.
- **$5,500–$9,000 ring target is realistic.** Setting budget fits comfortably with lab stone.

**Cons:**

- **Natural market is paper-thin.** Only 2 stones available. Both SI clarity.
- **The SI Clarity Trap.** Both natural stones require mandatory HD video check. SI2 risky, SI1 sometimes eye-clean.
- **No VVS natural options exist.** Buyers wanting natural VVS at 1.75ct must step up to 2ct or down to 1.5ct.
- **1.75ct is cutter-ignored.** Rough diamonds get optimized for 1.5ct or 2ct, not 1.75ct. Supply stays thin.
- **Width 6.5 mm is still narrow.** On size 7+ fingers, a halo helps anchor the ring visually.

### How to Beat the Cons

The natural scarcity solves by going lab (where inventory is deep enough) or jumping to 2ct natural (where VVS is available). The SI Clarity Trap solves with mandatory HD video review before purchase. The width problem solves with a halo (+2 mm perceived) or by picking a longer L:W ratio.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### How much does a 1.75 carat marquise diamond cost?

A 1.75 carat marquise diamond cost starts at **$1,638** for IGI E-VVS2 Excellent Cut lab-grown — four identical stones at this exact price. Natural starts at **$12,804** for GIA G-SI2 Very Good. The natural ceiling is $13,564 for E-SI1 Very Good — only 2 natural stones available at this tier.

### Does 1.74ct really save $900 vs 1.75ct?

Yes. A 1.74ct marquise sits one pricing tier below 1.75ct. The visual difference is 0.1 mm of length — invisible to the eye on a 13 mm stone. The pricing jump across the 1.75ct threshold runs 7–10%. On a $12,800 natural stone, that saves roughly $900 at 1.74ct for the same face-up appearance.

### What is the cheapest 1.75 carat marquise diamond?

The cheapest certified 1.75 carat marquise is any of four **[IGI E-VVS2 lab-grown stones at $1,638](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30162971?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)**. The cheapest natural is **[$12,804 GIA G-SI2 Very Good](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29498926?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)** — 7.8x the lab floor and SI clarity.

### Why is 1.75ct natural marquise only available in SI clarity?

Cutters producing natural marquise target 1.5ct and 2.0ct for commercial reasons. A 1.75ct marquise is often a stone that could not quite make 2.0ct and gets re-aimed at 1.75. These stones usually come with trade-offs — SI1 or SI2 clarity because the inclusion limited where the rough could be cut cleanly to 2ct+.

### Is lab or natural better at 1.75 carat marquise?

**Lab wins objective value by a 7.8x margin at 1.75ct.** $1,638 lab vs $12,804 natural for the same face-up size. The lab stones have better color (E vs G), better clarity (VVS2 vs SI2), and better cut label (Excellent/Ideal vs VG). Natural only makes sense for buyers with $14k+ ring budgets who want GIA + natural specifically.

### What clarity should I buy at 1.75 carat marquise?

**VVS2 is the clarity floor at 1.75ct lab.** Four stones at this spec sit at $1,638. On natural, only SI1 or SI2 is available — step up to 2ct natural or down to 1.5ct natural if you want VVS natural. SI1 is sometimes eye-clean on marquise; SI2 is often not. HD video check mandatory on all SI natural.

### What color grade is best for 1.75 carat marquise?

**E color is cheap on lab at 1.75ct — buy it.** The Lab Quadruplet is all E-VVS2 at $1,638. D color adds $1,728 for Flawless D-FL combo. For natural at 1.75ct, G is the floor and E is the ceiling — both only come in SI clarity right now. Full logic in our [marquise diamond color guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide).

### How big does a 1.75 carat marquise look on the finger?

A 1.75 carat marquise is 13.0 × 6.5 mm. On a size-6 finger it covers 79% of the width lengthwise. Face-up area is 66.4 mm² — reads as a 2.25 carat round diamond in apparent size thanks to the Elongation Illusion.

### Does a 1.75 carat marquise bow-tie?

Yes, and bow-tie at 1.75ct is more visible than at 1.5ct because the stone is bigger. Always check the HD video on the retailer listing. The Bow-Tie Amplifier is why Ideal cut matters at this size. A hard dark band across the center is a reject. See [marquise diamond bow-tie](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).

### What setting is best for 1.75 carat marquise?

**Platinum pavé halo** at $7,000 total budget. The halo adds 2 mm perceived width — a 1.75ct marquise with halo reads at 15 × 8.5 mm. Platinum keeps the stone colour clean. $5,362 setting budget (after $1,638 stone) fits platinum halo comfortably with accent diamonds. See [marquise halo engagement ring](/marquise-halo-engagement-ring) guide.

### Can I resell a 1.75 carat marquise diamond?

Resale at 1.75ct natural marquise returns **25–35% of retail** — below adjacent tiers because of the thin inventory and SI clarity profile. Lab resale is near zero. Buy for the ring, not the exit. If resale matters, scale to 2ct natural GIA where VVS inventory depth is better.

### What is the price per carat for 1.75 marquise?

At 1.75ct natural, price per carat runs **$7,316 to $7,751/ct** ($12,804–$13,564 divided by 1.75). Lab per-carat sits at **$936 to $2,515/ct** ($1,638–$4,402 divided by 1.75). Natural per-carat at 1.75ct is lower than 1.5ct natural ($8,393–$15,803/ct) — unusual tier pricing because SI clarity dominates the natural inventory.

### Is 1.75ct marquise worth it vs 2ct?

Depends on budget. Lab 1.75ct starts at $1,638 vs lab 2ct around $2,232 — $594 for 7% more face-up area at 2ct. Natural 1.75ct starts at $12,804 vs natural 2ct around $18,000 — $5,196 more for 2ct natural with VVS clarity options. If budget flexes, 2ct natural wins; if tight, 1.75ct lab wins.

### What length-to-width ratio is best for 1.75 marquise?

**1.90 to 2.00** is the sweet spot at 1.75ct. The classic 2.00 L:W gives 13.0 × 6.5 mm — the measurements this guide uses. Narrower than 1.90 amplifies bow-tie risk at this larger size. Wider than 2.10 thins the silhouette noticeably. Full logic in our [marquise L:W ratio guide](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio).

