## The Metal Match at 1.75ct — Picking Color by Metal First

Every color grade decision at 1.75ct should start with the metal choice. The metal sets the color-grade floor. Buyers who pick color first and metal second end up overpaying.

### Color Grade by Metal Match

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Metal</th><th>Minimum Color</th><th>Our Pick</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Platinum</strong></td><td>F (if natural) / E (if lab)</td><td>IGI E-VVS2 Ideal lab <a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30162971?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,638</a></td><td>Platinum shows warmth. E on lab or F+ on natural keeps the stone clean.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>14k White Gold</strong></td><td>F (if natural) / E (if lab)</td><td>IGI E-VVS2 Ideal lab <a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30162971?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,638</a></td><td>Same as platinum. Rhodium plating wears off every 2 years and the yellow underneath will highlight stone colour.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>14k Yellow Gold</strong></td><td>H (if natural) / E (lab, since cheap)</td><td>GIA G-SI2 Very Good natural <a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29498926?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$12,804</a></td><td>Warm metal hides warm stone. Natural G-SI2 works here if eye-clean verified.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>14k Rose Gold</strong></td><td>H (if natural) / E (lab)</td><td>Same lab E-VVS2 Ideal <a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30162971?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,638</a></td><td>Rose gold is forgiving. Lab E is overkill but priced the same as E, so take it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Platinum pavé halo</strong></td><td>E (lab)</td><td>IGI D-FL Ideal lab <a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30016416?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$3,366</a></td><td>Halo accent diamonds are usually G-H colour. Center needs to match or beat.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** On lab, E color is the floor across all metals because the Lab Quadruplet puts E-VVS2 at $1,638. On natural, the metal choice decides everything — platinum needs F+ (not available at 1.75ct natural right now), yellow or rose gold tolerates G-SI2 if eye-clean. Lab wins again for universal metal compatibility.

> **"My take at 1.75ct — if you're set on natural, you have two stones to pick from. Both are SI clarity. Both need HD video verification. If either fails the eye-clean check on video, you have no fallback at this carat tier. Lab gives you four floor options plus three max-spec options. That is why I always start the lab conversation first at 1.75ct."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

## Why 1.75ct Marquise Lab Beats 2ct Round Lab

A 2ct round lab diamond measures 8.2 mm across — face-up area of 52.8 mm². A 1.75ct marquise lab measures 13.0 × 6.5 mm — face-up area of 66.4 mm². The marquise beats the round on area by 26%. The marquise beats the round on length by 59%. The round costs 36% more at this spec on the lab market.

The Blue Nile listing for a 1.75ct E-VVS2 Ideal marquise at $1,638 is a configuration you cannot match in a round of the same carat tier. The marquise shape wins at every measurement that matters for perceived size on the hand.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Spec</th><th>1.75ct Marquise Lab</th><th>2ct Round Lab</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Face-up (mm)</strong></td><td>13.0 × 6.5</td><td>8.2 Ø</td><td>Marquise is 59% longer than the round's diameter.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Face-up area (mm²)</strong></td><td>66.4</td><td>52.8</td><td>Marquise wins area by 26%.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Starting price (lab E-VVS2 Ideal)</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30162971?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,638</a></td><td>~$2,200</td><td>Marquise costs 74% of round at the same spec.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Reads as</strong></td><td>"Dramatic engagement ring"</td><td>"Classic 2ct round"</td><td>Both read premium. Marquise reads as more distinctive.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** For "biggest-looking ring per dollar" at this budget, 1.75ct marquise lab beats 2ct round lab by every measure. Pay less, get more face-up area, get a more distinctive silhouette. Full shape comparison in our [round diamond vs marquise](/round-diamond-vs-marquise) guide.

I check this comparison for every client who comes in asking for "the biggest lab ring my budget can buy." Nine out of ten walk out with 1.75ct marquise instead of 2ct round. The face-up size convinces them. The saved money goes into platinum settings that lift the final ring value past what a bare 2ct round would ever deliver.

The one trade-off is finger coverage side-to-side. A 2ct round covers 50% of a size-6 finger across. A 1.75ct marquise covers 39% side-to-side but 79% lengthwise. If side-to-side width matters more to you (common on size 7+ fingers), round wins. If length matters more (common on size 5–6 or anyone wanting a dramatic silhouette), marquise wins. Both are honest buys at this price tier. The choice comes down to how your partner wears rings on the hand and which direction catches their eye more.

## How to Verify a 1.75 Carat Marquise Before You Buy

Three checks every stone must pass before you add to cart.

**Check 1 — The certificate measurements.** For a 1.75ct marquise at 2.00 L:W, measurements should read close to 13.0 × 6.5 × 4.0 mm. If length is below 12.6 mm, the stone is cut deep and wasting carat weight below the girdle. More in our [marquise ideal proportions guide](/marquise-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

**Check 2 — The HD video for bow-tie and eye-clean verification.** Open the 360° HD video on the retailer listing. For lab stones, verify bow-tie is faint. For natural SI stones, verify no visible inclusion in the center of the table — this is the SI Clarity Trap check. Hard dark band or center inclusion = walk away.

**Check 3 — L:W ratio inside 1.85–2.10.** Divide length by width. Outside that range at 1.75ct, bow-tie amplifies and the silhouette gets off-balance. Reject if outside range.

I run these three checks on every 1.75ct marquise before I quote a client. The sub-threshold trick and the Lab Quadruplet floor are both value plays — but the stone itself must pass these three physical checks first. A $900 saving on a stone that fails bow-tie check or shows a center-of-table SI inclusion is not actually a saving. It is a worse ring at a lower price. The checks come first. The value plays come second.

